*(
    title: The Cell Membrane
    description: A double layer of lipid with proteins floating in it, and why that structure decides what can cross.
    tags: cell membrane, phospholipid bilayer, fluid mosaic, module 1, biology, stub
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    A cell must be separated from its surroundings or its contents would disperse, but a perfect seal would starve it. The membrane resolves the conflict by being selectively permeable, and it can do that because of what it is made of.

    Written out here: the phospholipid bilayer and why molecules with a water-loving head and a water-fearing tail arrange themselves that way on their own, the fluid mosaic model, and the channel and carrier proteins embedded in it.

    What the structure permits is the next question: which substances cross, in which direction, and at what cost to the cell. That is *Transport Across Membranes*.

    internote(ref: transport-across-membranes.chalk)
}
